Every app also gets:

- `SSL_CERT_FILE`: the lab's trust bundle. Go's `crypto/tls` reads it, so clients verify every server with no certificate code.
- `BB_SERVICE` and `BB_FQDN`: this app's name and address.
- `TLS_CERT_FILE` and `TLS_KEY_FILE`: this app's certificate and key, for serving TLS yourself.

## Run it

```sh
bb up                 # build the image, start the lab, wait for health
bb logs {{.Name}}     # confirm every client connected
bb creds              # every variable, with values
bb status             # state and health of every service
```

{{if .Uses}}`main()` calls `requireEnv` with the variables of the services in `uses`, listed above, then connects each client and verifies connectivity (ping/check) before waiting on `SIGTERM`. If a variable is missing, the program stops with one message naming each one.{{else}}`main()` checks nothing yet (`requireEnv()`), logs ready, and waits on `SIGTERM`.{{end}} It does not check `SSL_CERT_FILE`, `BB_SERVICE`, `BB_FQDN`, `TLS_CERT_FILE` and `TLS_KEY_FILE`: Breadboard supplies the app's own TLS and identity variables to every app.

## Change it

Write your experiment in `main.go` right below the connected clients (and delete any helper you don't need, such as a Kafka producer or consumer), then run `bb up` again. It rebuilds the image (only what changed recompiles) and restarts the app.

`go.mod` lists every client library Breadboard's starters use, so adding a service needs no download step. Run `go mod tidy` to keep only the ones you import. The module path is `example.com/{{.Name}}`, so no standard library package can shadow it whatever the app is called; rename it if you publish the code. The binary and the command stay `{{.Name}}`.

## Add a service

{{if .Example}}```sh
bb add {{.Example}}          # bb catalog lists every type
```

Then list it in this app's `uses` in `experiment.yaml`:

```yaml
apps:
  {{.Name}}:
    uses: [{{range .Uses}}{{.Service}}, {{end}}{{.Example}}]
```

Run `bb up`, and `bb creds` shows the new variables. `main.go` was written once, so add the new variables to `requireEnv` and wire the client yourself; `bb learn {{.Example}}` explains the env contract.{{else}}```sh
bb add <type> --name <new-name>    # bb catalog lists every type
```

Then add `<new-name>` to this app's `uses` in `experiment.yaml`; each entry appears once. Run `bb up`, and `bb creds` shows the new variables. `main.go` was written once, so add the new variables to `requireEnv` and wire the client yourself; `bb learn <type>` explains the env contract.{{end}} When two services of the same type are in `uses`, the later one's variables are prefixed with its name, such as `ANALYTICS_DATABASE_URL`.

## Reach it from your browser

The app is private to the lab by default. Two ways in:

- `ports: [8080]` in the app's entry publishes a container port on `127.0.0.1:8080`.
- `host: {port: 8443}` serves the app at `https://{{.Name}}.{{.Experiment}}.localhost:<port>` through the lab's host router. The app must serve TLS on that port, with `TLS_CERT_FILE` and `TLS_KEY_FILE`. `bb up` prints the URL; `bb ca --host` explains how to trust it.

## Without Breadboard

The program needs only its environment variables and a trust bundle, so it runs anywhere they exist. `docker build --target prod -t {{.Name}} .` builds a small non-root image.
